This project re-implements ideas from the projects below in its own TypeScript code.
No source code was copied from any of them.

Ideas and attributions
----------------------

llmfit (MIT License)
  The quantisation walk-down order (QUANT_HIERARCHY, best quant for a memory budget) used by
  the "llmfit-style installed-only weights estimate" baseline, and labelling every figure as
  measured versus estimated. Our baseline is weights-only and is not a faithful llmfit replica.

dalist1/ollama-bench (MIT License)
  Deterministic benchmark settings (fixed seed, capped tokens), client-side time to first
  token, and first-answer latency for thinking models.

rockyRunnr/ollama-bench (MIT License)
  Saving raw JSON results and comparing runs; use of Ollama's eval_count / eval_duration
  fields. (Its use of process RSS as memory is deliberately not followed; /api/ps is used.)

llama.cpp llama-bench (MIT License)
  Repeated measurements with summary statistics and machine-readable output, as a shape
  reference only.

quant-sim (ideas only)
  The overall per-quant speed + memory + quality + recommend concept and offline-by-default
  behaviour. Its recommend rule is what this project replaces (paired test, no least-bad
  fallback). Ideas only; no code or text used.

IFEval (Apache License 2.0)
  Jeffrey Zhou et al., "Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models" (2023).
  Methodology inspiration for the six instruction-following items (verifiable, parameterised
  rules), which are original items written for this project (MIT). They were not sampled from
  IFEval; no IFEval prompts, data or code are included.

mulberry32
  Public-domain PRNG algorithm by Tommy Ettinger, re-implemented in src/core/evalkit/prng.ts.

Datasets
--------

data/items.v1.json includes the exact text of 18 dataset rows, fetched on 2026-10-24 from the
public Hugging Face datasets-server rows API (no account). Each item records its sourceId
("<dataset>:<config>/<split>#<rowIndex>"), the dataset revision reported at fetch time, the
request URL and the row index. The row texts are kept verbatim (GSM8K rows 0-9 of the 12 requested are used) in
data/sources/fetched-20261024.json. Items are the question (plus lettered choices for MMLU)
followed by a fixed answer-format instruction written for this project.

GSM8K (MIT License)
  Copyright (c) 2021 OpenAI. Karl Cobbe et al., "Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word
  Problems" (2021). https://huggingface.co/datasets/openai/gsm8k (config main, split test,
  rows 0-9, revision 740312add88f781978c0658806c59bc2815b9866). Expected answers are the
  number after "####" in each row.

MMLU (MIT License)
  Copyright (c) 2020 Dan Hendrycks. Dan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Steven Basart, Andy Zou,
  Mantas Mazeika, Dawn Song and Jacob Steinhardt, "Measuring Massive Multitask Language
  Understanding" (ICLR 2021). https://huggingface.co/datasets/cais/mmlu (config all, split
  test, rows 0, 1750, 3500, 5250, 7000, 8750, 10500, 12250, revision
  c30699e8356da336a370243923dbaf21066bb9fe).

The six instruction items, the six extraction items, the three speed prompts and the 80
synthetic grader responses in fixtures/graders/ are original to this project (MIT).
